The Independent recently asked three of Britain’s leading children’s authors and
two of their in-house book experts to each pick 10 books, suitable for Year 7
students. Their original list is
[here](http://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/books/news/the-50-books-every-child-should-read-2250138.html).

Unfortunately, in typical traditional-media fashion, they neglected to add any
links to any of the books mentioned, losing an obvious opportunity to use the
web to both their readers’ and their own advantage. So I have gone through the
list and added links to the Kindle editions of every book for which one is
available.

I linked to a free edition wherever possible. If a book was not available on the
Kindle store, I marked it with an •color["red"]{\*} asterisk. If you know of a
Kindle version of a book I could not find, or of a free or better-formatted
edition than the one I have linked, let me know via email or comments!

## Philip Pullman

- [*Through the Looking
  Glass*](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B003B657NK/?tag=thloya-20) by Lewis Carroll.
  Indispensable. The great classic beginning of English children’s literature.
- [*Pinocchio*](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B000JQULUE/?tag=thloya-20)
  •color["red"]{(free)} by Carlo Collodi. What effortless invention looks like.
- [*Emil and the
  Detectives*](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0045Y15UQ/?tag=thloya-20) by Erich
  Kastner. A great political story: democracy in action.
- *[Swallows and Amazons](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B00R7TESJ6/?tag=thloya-20)*
  by Arthur Ransome. As clear and pure as Mozart.
- [*Black Hearts in
  Battersea*](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B003KK5DOK/?tag=thloya-20) by Joan
  Aiken. If Ransome was Mozart, Aiken was Rossini. Unforced effervescence.
- *[The Owl Service](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B00DPZN3AQ/?tag=thloya-20)* by
  Alan Garner. Showed how children’s literature could sound dark and troubling
  chords.
- [*The Phantom Tollbooth*](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B004IK8Q90/?tag=thloya-20)
  by Norton Juster. Superb wit and vigorous invention.
- *[Moominsummer Madness](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B00MLMS9SA/?tag=thloya-20)*
  by Tove Jansson. Any of the Moomin books would supply the same strange light
  Nordic magic.
- *[A Hundred Million
  Francs](https://www.amazon.com/dp/0370009428/?tag=thloya-20)•color["red"]{\*}*
  by Paul Berna. A particular favourite of mine, as much for Richard Kennedy’s
  delicate illustrations (in the English edition) as for the story.
- [*The Castafiore
  Emerald*](https://www.amazon.com/dp/0316358428/?tag=thloya-20)•color["red"]{\*}
  by Hergé. Three generations of this family have loved Tintin. Perfect timing,
  perfect narrative tact and command, blissfully funny.

## Michael Morpurgo

- [*The Star of Kazan*](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B003GGSTQ2/?tag=thloya-20) by
  Eva Ibbotson. The heroine is blessed with such wonderful friends who help her
  through the twists and turns of this incredible journey.
- [*A Christmas Carol*](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B000JQUKKU/?tag=thloya-20)
  •color["red"]{(free)} by Charles Dickens. The first few pages were so
  engaging, Marley’s ghostly face on the knocker of Scrooge’s door still gives
  me the shivers.
- [*Just William*](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B003GK21NK/?tag=thloya-20) books by
  Richmal Crompton. These are a must for every child.
- [*The Happy Prince*](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B000JQV2K2/?tag=thloya-20)
  •color["red"]{(free)} by Oscar Wilde. This was the first story, I think, that
  ever made me cry and it still has the power to make me cry.
- [*The Just So Stories*](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B002ZCZ67S/?tag=thloya-20)
  by Rudyard Kipling. The story my mother used to read me most often, because I
  asked for it again and again. I loved the sheer fun of it, the music and the
  rhythm of the words. It was subversive too. Still my favourite story.
- [*Treasure Island*](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B000JML7EC/?tag=thloya-20)
  •color["red"]{(free)} by R.L. Stevenson This was the first real book I read
  for myself. I lived this book as I read it.
- [*The Old Man and the
  Sea*](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B000FC0SH8/?tag=thloya-20) by Ernest
  Hemingway. A classic tale of man versus nature. I wish I’d written this.
- [*The Man Who Planted
  Trees*](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B00SBSWA10/?tag=thloya-20) by Jean Giono. A
  book for children from 8 to 80. I love the humanity of this story and how one
  man’s efforts can change the future for so many.
- [*The Singing
  Tree*](https://www.amazon.com/dp/0140345434/?tag=thloya-20)•color["red"]{\*}
  by Kate Seredy. The story of two children who go to find their father who has
  been listed missing in the trenches of the First World War.
- [*The Secret Garden*](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B002RKSZT4/?tag=thloya-20)
  •color["red"]{(free)} by Frances Hodgson-Burnett. I love this story of a
  girl’s life being changed by nature.

## Katy Guest, literary editor for The Independent on Sunday

- [*Refugee Boy*](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0058RE06K/?tag=thloya-20) by
  Benjamin Zephaniah. Story of a young Ethiopian boy, whose parents abandon him
  in London to save his life.
- [*Finn Family
  Moomintroll*](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B00MLM9KQU/?tag=thloya-20) (and the
  other Moomin books) by Tove Jansson. A fantasy series for small children that
  introduces bigger ones to ideas of adventure, dealing with fear, understanding
  character and tolerating difference.
- *[Diary of a Wimpy Kid](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B005CRQ4OW/?tag=thloya-20)*
  by Jeff Kinney. It’s rude, it’s funny and it will chime with every 11-year-old
  who’s ever started a new school.
- *[I Capture the Castle](https://www.amazon.com/dp/031231616X/?tag=thloya-20)*
  by Dodie Smith. Written for a teenage audience but fun at any age.
- *[The Lord of the Rings](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0026REBFK/?tag=thloya-20)*
  by J.R.R. Tolkein. Be warned, these tales of hobbits, elves and Middle Earth
  are dangerously addictive.
- *[The Tygrine
  Cat](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0048ELCL0/?tag=thloya-20)•color["red"]{\*}*
  (and *The Tygrine Cat on the Run*) by Inbali Iserles. If your parents keep
  going on at you to read *Tarka the Otter, The Sheep-Pig* and other animal
  fantasies, do—they’re great books—also try Iserles’ stories about a cat
  seeking his destiny.
- *[Carry On, Jeeves](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B00OR7XF9U/?tag=thloya-20)* by
  P.G. Wodehouse. A grown-up book ? but not that grown-up.
- *[When Hitler Stole Pink
  Rabbit](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B001QWDRZ6/?tag=thloya-20)* by Judith Kerr.
  Judith Kerr’s semi-autobiographical story of a family fleeing the Nazis
  in 1933.
- *[Moving Pictures](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B000UVBT3G/?tag=thloya-20)* by
  Terry Pratchett. Elaborate mythological imagery and a background based in real
  science. If you like this, the Discworld series offers plenty more.
- *[The Story of Tracy
  Beaker](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B001V7U6GY/?tag=thloya-20)* by Jacqueline
  Wilson. The pinnacle of the wonderful Jacqueline Wilson’s brilliant and
  enormous output.

## John Walsh, author and Independent columnist

- *[The Adventures of Sherlock
  Holmes](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B000JQU1VS/?tag=thloya-20)*
  •color["red"]{(free)} by Sir Arthur Conan Doyle. Irresistible puzzle-solving
  tales of the chilly Victorian master-sleuth and his dim medical sidekick.
- *[The Curious Incident of the Dog in the
  Night-Time](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B000FC1MCS/?tag=thloya-20)* by Mark
  Haddon. Age-transcending tale, both funny and sad.
- *[Mistress Masham’s
  Repose](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B005SGW806/?tag=thloya-20)* by T.H. White.
  Magical story of 10-year-old Maria, living in a derelict stately home, shy,
  lonely and under threat from both her governess and her rascally guardian.
- *[Little Women](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B000JQUMPI/?tag=thloya-20)*
  •color["red"]{(free)} by Louisa May Alcott. Inexplicably evergreen, trend and
  taste-defying 1868 classic.
- *[How to be
  Topp](https://www.amazon.com/dp/1851459650/?tag=thloya-20)•color["red"]{\*}*
  by Geoffrey Willams and Ronald Searle. Side-splitting satire on skool, oiks,
  teechers, fules, bulies, swots.
- *[Stormbreaker](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B000TU16GS/?tag=thloya-20)* by
  Anthony Horowitz. First of the action-packed adventures with 14-year-old Alex
  Rider.
- *[Private Peaceful](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B00I11F24Y/?tag=thloya-20)* by
  Michael Morpurgo. “Dulce et Decorum Est” for pre-teens.
- *[Artemis Fowl](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B002KP6DXQ/?tag=thloya-20)* by Eoin
  Colfer. Lively, amoral, wildly imaginative debut (six more followed) about the
  money-grabbing master-criminal Artemis, 12. The author called it “*Die Hard*
  with fairies.”
- *[The Silver
  Sword](https://www.amazon.com/dp/0099572850/?tag=thloya-20)•color["red"]{\*}*
  by Ian Serraillier. Inspiring wartime story of the Balicki family in Warsaw.
- *[Animal Farm](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B004G8P0UW/?tag=thloya-20)*
  •color["red"]{(free)} by George Orwell. Smart 11-year-olds won’t need any
  pre-knowledge of Marx, Lenin, Trotsky and 1917 to appreciate this
  brilliantly-told fable.

## Michael Rosen

- *[Skellig](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B000FC1KGG/?tag=thloya-20)* by David
  Almond. Brings magical realism to working-class North-east England.
- *[Red Cherry
  Red](https://www.amazon.com/dp/0747589798/?tag=thloya-20)•color["red"]{\*}* by
  Jackie Kay. A book of poems that reaches deep into our hidden thoughts but
  also talks in a joyous voice exploring the everyday.
- *[Talking Turkeys](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B00358VI28/?tag=thloya-20)* by
  Benjamin Zephaniah. A book of poems that demands to be read aloud, performed
  and thought about.
- *[Greek
  myths](https://www.amazon.com/dp/1852133732/?tag=thloya-20)•color["red"]{\*}*
  by Geraldine McCaughrean. Superheroes battle with demons, gods intervene in
  our pleasures and fears—a bit like the spectres in our minds going through
  daily life, really—beautifully retold here.
- *[People Might Hear You (Viking Kestrel picture
  books)](https://www.amazon.com/dp/0670803030/?tag=thloya-20)
  •color["red"]{\*}* by Robin Klein. A profound, suspenseful story about sects,
  freedom and the rights of all young people—especially girls.
- *[Noughts and Crosses](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B002RKSPE4/?tag=thloya-20)*
  •color["red"]{(free)} by Malorie Blackman. A book that dared to go where no
  one thought you could with young audiences because it raises tough stuff to do
  with race.
- *Einstein’s Underpants and How They Saved the World* by Anthony McGowan. A
  crazy adventure set amongst the kids you don’t want to know but who this book
  makes you really, really care about.
- *[After the First Death](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B004GTLSG6/?tag=thloya-20)*
  by Robert Cormier. Cormier is never afraid of handling how the personal meets
  the political all within the framework of a thriller.
- *[The London Eye
  Mystery](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0013TPUNO/?tag=thloya-20)* by Siobhan
  Dowd. A book that allows difference to be part of the plot and not a point in
  itself.
- *Beano Annual.* A cornucopia of nutty, bad, silly ideas, tricks, situations
  and plots

*•color["red"]{\*}—not known to be available as an ebook.*
